- expert\_quote (verbatim, full source text; use whole or as word-for-word extracts; never paraphrase or change the meaning):

Nails probably do grow a little more slowly during the winter, but the difference is pretty small. Fingernails typically grow around 3 mm per month, so any seasonal change is usually subtle.

Cold weather may play a role because the small blood vessels in your fingers constrict when you're cold. That means slightly less blood flow to the nail matrix, which is where the nail actually grows. Vitamin D, on the other hand, doesn't seem to have much of an effect on nail growth.

What people tend to notice more in the winter is that their nails become dry and brittle. Cold air, indoor heating, frequent hand washing, and hand sanitizer can all dry out the nail. When nails start splitting or breaking, it can seem like they aren't growing when they actually are.

A few simple things can help:

Keep your hands warm and wear gloves when you're outside. Use a thick hand cream or plain petrolatum after washing your hands, and rub some into the nails and cuticles as well. Wear gloves when washing dishes or cleaning, and try not to overdo acetone, gel manicures, or gel removal.

Nutrition matters too. Make sure you're eating enough protein. If there has been a noticeable change in your nails, I'd also consider checking things like iron levels and thyroid function, since those are two potentially correctable causes of nail changes that come up fairly often.

There are a few supplements that may be worth considering as well.

Collagen peptides may help with brittle nails. There are some early studies suggesting that taking collagen consistently may modestly improve nail growth and decrease breakage over several months.

GHK-Cu is a copper peptide involved in collagen production and tissue repair. Most of the research has focused on skin and hair rather than nails specifically, so I would think of this as potentially supportive rather than something that has strong evidence for nail growth.

Biotin can sometimes help people who have genuinely brittle or splitting nails. One important thing to know is that biotin can interfere with certain blood tests, including some thyroid and cardiac tests, so it's worth mentioning if you're taking it before having labs drawn.

If you're noticing a significant change in the shape, color, texture, or thickness of your nails, that's a little different and is worth taking a closer look at during your next visit.

